1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,350
Every few days, another
serious bug makes the news.

2
00:00:04,170 --> 00:00:06,370
A bug that lets someone skip the login.

3
00:00:06,970 --> 00:00:08,850
A bug that leaks private data.

4
00:00:09,630 --> 00:00:11,970
A bug that hands an
attacker your computer.

5
00:00:13,090 --> 00:00:16,370
Sometimes it's Windows. Sometimes Linux.

6
00:00:16,850 --> 00:00:19,810
A browser, a database, a cloud service...

7
00:00:20,190 --> 00:00:23,710
or a small library that millions
of developers depend on.

8
00:00:24,470 --> 00:00:27,030
And the reaction is
almost always the same.

9
00:00:27,650 --> 00:00:29,530
How did the programmers miss this?

10
00:00:30,160 --> 00:00:31,360
These are professionals.

11
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:35,940
They have code reviews, automated
tests, security audits, fuzzing...

12
00:00:36,380 --> 00:00:38,240
and the bugs keep coming anyway.

13
00:00:39,230 --> 00:00:42,910
So is it even possible to write
complex software without bugs?

14
00:00:44,390 --> 00:00:45,990
Let's start with a number.

15
00:00:46,630 --> 00:00:51,290
Imagine a program with just a hundred
settings, each of them either on or off.

16
00:00:52,250 --> 00:00:54,650
A hundred simple yes-or-no decisions.

17
00:00:55,550 --> 00:00:57,510
How many combinations is that?

18
00:00:58,080 --> 00:00:59,860
Two to the power of a hundred.

19
00:01:00,380 --> 00:01:02,620
That's roughly a million
trillion trillion.

20
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:07,440
Now imagine you could test a
billion combinations every second.

21
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:09,900
And you started at the Big Bang.

22
00:01:10,750 --> 00:01:15,197
By today, you'd have tested about
one combination in three thousand.

23
00:01:15,497 --> 00:01:17,477
And a hundred switches is nothing.

24
00:01:18,077 --> 00:01:20,457
A real program has millions of them.

25
00:01:20,637 --> 00:01:25,177
So "just test everything" isn't
expensive. It's impossible.

26
00:01:27,737 --> 00:01:29,777
And those switches aren't exotic.

27
00:01:30,437 --> 00:01:33,497
Take one of the simplest
things a program ever does.

28
00:01:34,077 --> 00:01:36,877
A user asks for their
account by its number.

29
00:01:37,337 --> 00:01:40,777
We find it in the
database. We send it back.

30
00:01:41,547 --> 00:01:43,547
But what if the user doesn't exist?

31
00:01:44,347 --> 00:01:47,107
What if someone sends
text instead of a number?

32
00:01:47,267 --> 00:01:49,067
Or a negative number?

33
00:01:49,147 --> 00:01:50,887
Or an enormous one?

34
00:01:51,587 --> 00:01:54,167
What if this person
isn't allowed to see it?

35
00:01:54,647 --> 00:01:58,707
What if someone deletes the account
at the exact moment we're loading it?

36
00:01:58,907 --> 00:02:01,487
What if the same request arrives twice?

37
00:02:02,577 --> 00:02:04,857
Every one of these questions is simple.

38
00:02:05,417 --> 00:02:07,137
The problem is how they combine.

39
00:02:07,897 --> 00:02:09,497
Each one is another switch.

40
00:02:10,417 --> 00:02:13,897
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

41
00:02:14,517 --> 00:02:16,137
It really helps the channel.

42
00:02:18,307 --> 00:02:20,847
And a computer won't sort them out for us.

43
00:02:21,307 --> 00:02:25,267
It does exactly what we tell
it to. Not what we meant.

44
00:02:25,897 --> 00:02:28,557
That's why we invented
programming languages.

45
00:02:29,357 --> 00:02:33,337
They let us describe complex things
without thinking about every detail.

46
00:02:34,377 --> 00:02:38,137
But some of them take care of
less than you'd hope. Take C.

47
00:02:38,797 --> 00:02:42,337
It's fast, it's simple, and it gives
you enormous control over memory.

48
00:02:43,647 --> 00:02:47,747
So you can write this. We reserve
room for sixteen characters.

49
00:02:48,527 --> 00:02:50,607
Then we say: copy the input here.

50
00:02:51,387 --> 00:02:55,887
What if the input is five hundred
characters long? C won't ask.

51
00:02:56,267 --> 00:02:58,467
It just keeps writing past the end.

52
00:02:59,347 --> 00:03:01,867
And what's right after
those sixteen characters?

53
00:03:02,567 --> 00:03:04,467
Often, something very important.

54
00:03:05,297 --> 00:03:07,597
When a program calls a
function, it writes down

55
00:03:07,827 --> 00:03:12,117
where to go back once the function
is done. A return address.

56
00:03:13,037 --> 00:03:16,577
In a classic layout, it sits not
far past the end of our buffer.

57
00:03:17,517 --> 00:03:20,877
So an attacker sends an input
of exactly the right length.

58
00:03:21,437 --> 00:03:23,077
First, their own instructions.

59
00:03:23,977 --> 00:03:26,437
Then, at the very end,
a new return address...

60
00:03:26,897 --> 00:03:28,637
pointing back at those instructions.

61
00:03:29,757 --> 00:03:34,077
The function finishes. The processor
looks up where to return...

62
00:03:34,437 --> 00:03:36,857
and jumps straight into
the attacker's code.

63
00:03:37,717 --> 00:03:40,937
Now it's running their program,
with all the permissions of yours.

64
00:03:41,737 --> 00:03:44,557
That's what "executing
arbitrary code" means.

65
00:03:45,527 --> 00:03:49,827
And the processor didn't do anything
wrong. It followed the instructions.

66
00:03:50,967 --> 00:03:54,807
In 1988, a mistake
exactly like this let the

67
00:03:55,037 --> 00:03:57,587
Morris worm spread across
the early internet.

68
00:03:58,467 --> 00:04:01,507
And the same trick has been
used in attacks ever since.

69
00:04:04,317 --> 00:04:07,617
So newer languages hand part
of the job to the compiler.

70
00:04:08,157 --> 00:04:12,837
Rust, for example, refuses to build
many programs that could misuse memory.

71
00:04:13,987 --> 00:04:17,107
The programmer says: but I
know exactly what I'm doing!

72
00:04:17,407 --> 00:04:20,387
And the compiler says:
that's what everyone says.

73
00:04:21,257 --> 00:04:23,137
And it's right to be suspicious.

74
00:04:23,997 --> 00:04:27,897
Microsoft and Google's Chrome team
have both reported that around seventy

75
00:04:28,127 --> 00:04:32,417
percent of the security bugs they've
studied come from memory safety problems.

76
00:04:33,507 --> 00:04:37,487
But a program can be perfectly
memory-safe and still completely wrong.

77
00:04:38,427 --> 00:04:39,747
Imagine a user logs in.

78
00:04:39,977 --> 00:04:45,687
Their password is checked correctly.
Nothing crashes. No memory is corrupted.

79
00:04:46,467 --> 00:04:49,447
And then the program shows
them somebody else's data.

80
00:04:49,627 --> 00:04:52,507
Because the permission check
was written slightly wrong.

81
00:04:53,437 --> 00:04:55,957
No language can catch that on its own.

82
00:04:56,597 --> 00:05:01,617
The language doesn't know what the program
was supposed to do. Only we know that.

83
00:05:03,877 --> 00:05:09,217
So back to testing. A test can tell
you: for this input, I got this result.

84
00:05:09,907 --> 00:05:13,147
It can't tell you: the program
is right for every input.

85
00:05:13,227 --> 00:05:16,447
And we've already seen
why we can't try them all.

86
00:05:17,197 --> 00:05:22,337
In 1970, the computer scientist Edsger
Dijkstra put it in one sentence.

87
00:05:23,017 --> 00:05:26,577
Testing can show the presence of
bugs, but never their absence.

88
00:05:29,536 --> 00:05:32,496
Fine. So instead of testing,
why not write a program that

89
00:05:32,726 --> 00:05:35,456
checks other programs and
proves they're correct?

90
00:05:36,056 --> 00:05:40,036
Feed it any code, and it tells you
whether that code does what it should.

91
00:05:40,836 --> 00:05:44,916
That question was answered before the
first real computer was even built.

92
00:05:45,656 --> 00:05:50,536
In 1936, Alan Turing proved that
a program like that cannot exist.

93
00:05:51,726 --> 00:05:53,946
Not that nobody has built it yet.

94
00:05:54,186 --> 00:05:56,066
That it can't exist at all.

95
00:05:56,786 --> 00:05:59,106
He showed that no program
can decide, for every

96
00:05:59,336 --> 00:06:02,046
other program, whether it
will ever stop running.

97
00:06:03,146 --> 00:06:06,246
And in 1953, Henry Rice went further.

98
00:06:07,106 --> 00:06:08,906
The same is true for every non-trivial

99
00:06:08,986 --> 00:06:11,646
question about what a
program actually does.

100
00:06:12,686 --> 00:06:14,806
Is it secure? Does it leak data?

101
00:06:15,526 --> 00:06:17,206
Does it give the right answer?

102
00:06:17,806 --> 00:06:21,306
There is no general method that
settles that for every program.

103
00:06:23,876 --> 00:06:27,416
So is bug-free software
impossible? Not quite.

104
00:06:27,996 --> 00:06:30,096
For one specific program,
you can sometimes

105
00:06:30,326 --> 00:06:32,636
prove it correct, with enormous effort.

106
00:06:33,506 --> 00:06:37,886
In 2009, a team in Australia proved
that a small operating system

107
00:06:38,116 --> 00:06:43,166
kernel, called seL4, does exactly
what its specification says.

108
00:06:44,306 --> 00:06:46,666
It was under ten thousand lines of code.

109
00:06:47,606 --> 00:06:50,196
Building the kernel took
about two person-years.

110
00:06:50,566 --> 00:06:52,566
Proving it correct took about twenty.

111
00:06:53,046 --> 00:06:56,366
A modern web browser has
tens of millions of lines.

112
00:06:57,196 --> 00:06:59,196
And even that proof has a catch.

113
00:06:59,696 --> 00:07:02,196
It shows the program
matches its specification.

114
00:07:03,236 --> 00:07:06,756
It can't show that the specification
is what we actually wanted.

115
00:07:07,866 --> 00:07:10,526
And the specification
is written by people.

116
00:07:10,886 --> 00:07:12,966
So the question comes back to us.

117
00:07:15,416 --> 00:07:17,376
And it isn't only our own code.

118
00:07:18,016 --> 00:07:22,156
A programmer today builds on frameworks,
libraries and hundreds of dependencies.

119
00:07:23,256 --> 00:07:25,916
Every layer saves us
enormous amounts of work.

120
00:07:26,276 --> 00:07:30,756
But every layer is something we have
to understand... or at least trust.

121
00:07:31,416 --> 00:07:34,736
Which means you don't have to write
a bug to have one in your software.

122
00:07:35,216 --> 00:07:37,216
You can simply install it.

123
00:07:37,976 --> 00:07:42,836
Then add the pressure. Nobody wants
a perfect product in ten years.

124
00:07:43,456 --> 00:07:45,456
They want a working one next month.

125
00:07:45,916 --> 00:07:49,696
And a break-in that never happened is
very hard to show in a presentation.

126
00:07:51,106 --> 00:07:52,966
And here's the strangest part.

127
00:07:53,406 --> 00:07:55,146
Our tools keep getting better.

128
00:07:55,286 --> 00:07:58,166
Safer languages, fuzzing, bug bounties...

129
00:07:59,066 --> 00:08:02,066
And yet the number of known
vulnerabilities keeps growing.

130
00:08:02,686 --> 00:08:07,186
In 2024 alone, around forty
thousand got an official number.

131
00:08:07,806 --> 00:08:11,586
Partly because we're also writing
vastly more software than ever.

132
00:08:11,906 --> 00:08:14,146
It's like making every road safer...

133
00:08:14,446 --> 00:08:17,026
while building millions
of times more roads.

134
00:08:19,376 --> 00:08:22,796
So are programmers bad at their jobs? No.

135
00:08:23,296 --> 00:08:26,956
We've been asking them for something
that no general method can guarantee.

136
00:08:28,036 --> 00:08:32,916
So maybe "how do we write software
without bugs?" is the wrong question.

137
00:08:33,746 --> 00:08:36,106
The better question is:
how do we build systems

138
00:08:36,336 --> 00:08:39,286
that stay safe even when
the software has bugs?

139
00:08:42,106 --> 00:08:44,726
And that's exactly where
security is heading.

140
00:08:45,426 --> 00:08:47,466
It doesn't say: never make a mistake.

141
00:08:47,546 --> 00:08:49,886
It says: assume you already have.

142
00:08:50,446 --> 00:08:52,126
Remember that return address?

143
00:08:52,926 --> 00:08:54,626
Today, the system guards it.

144
00:08:54,706 --> 00:08:57,366
It shuffles where things sit in memory.

145
00:08:58,026 --> 00:09:00,966
And it refuses to run
instructions hidden in data.

146
00:09:02,046 --> 00:09:04,386
The bug in our C code is still there.

147
00:09:04,806 --> 00:09:07,366
It's just much harder
to turn into an attack.

148
00:09:08,256 --> 00:09:10,116
And the same idea is everywhere.

149
00:09:10,956 --> 00:09:13,156
Sandboxes, limited permissions, isolated

150
00:09:13,386 --> 00:09:16,436
processes, encryption,
backups, monitoring...

151
00:09:16,516 --> 00:09:20,456
layer after layer, so that one
bug doesn't become a disaster.

152
00:09:23,206 --> 00:09:25,526
And it has a very practical consequence.

153
00:09:26,206 --> 00:09:29,286
If bugs are inevitable,
fixing them isn't optional.

154
00:09:30,216 --> 00:09:32,076
The software you're using today may well

155
00:09:32,306 --> 00:09:35,156
contain a vulnerability
nobody has found yet.

156
00:09:35,956 --> 00:09:38,636
And when someone does, the
update is the difference between

157
00:09:38,866 --> 00:09:42,076
"a vulnerability was discovered"
and "we were hacked".

158
00:09:43,006 --> 00:09:45,346
The same goes for the systems we build.

159
00:09:46,106 --> 00:09:50,426
Old libraries and unsupported platforms
don't get safer by being ignored.

160
00:09:51,086 --> 00:09:52,866
They turn into technical debt.

161
00:09:53,496 --> 00:09:57,416
And why that debt has to be paid off
is what my video "It Still Works.

162
00:09:58,096 --> 00:10:00,216
So Why Upgrade It?" is about.

163
00:10:00,654 --> 00:10:03,094
Because in software, "it
still works" and "it's

164
00:10:03,324 --> 00:10:05,304
still safe" are two very different things.
